      {
        "id": "thm-conforming-galerkin-eigenspace-gap-under-isolated-cluster",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Conforming Galerkin invariant subspaces converge across an isolated cluster",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-eigenspace-best-approximation-gap",
          "def-compact-linear-operator",
          "def-operator-norm",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "lem-compact-self-adjoint-residual-controls-invariant-subspace-gap",
          "lem-conforming-energy-projection-is-best-approximation",
          "lem-finite-dimensional-galerkin-minmax",
          "thm-compact-coercive-form-spectrum-and-minmax",
          "thm-conforming-galerkin-eigenvalue-upper-bound-quadratic-gap-error",
          "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "lem-compactness-is-intrinsic",
          "thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded",
          "lem-closed-subset-of-a-compact-space-is-compact"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the compact coercive form setting of\n[[thm-compact-coercive-form-spectrum-and-minmax]]. Suppose a continuous\neigenvalue $\\lambda$ occupies the isolated cluster of indices\n$r,\\ldots,s$, so $\\lambda_r=\\cdots=\\lambda_s=\\lambda$ and\n$\\lambda_{s+1}>\\lambda$, with $\\lambda_{r-1}<\\lambda$ when $r>1$. Let\n$E:=\\operatorname{span}\\{e_r,\\ldots,e_s\\}$,\n$E_s:=\\operatorname{span}\\{e_1,\\ldots,e_s\\}$, and let\n$V_h\\subseteq V$ be conforming with $\\dim V_h\\ge s$. Put\n$\\delta_s:=\\delta_a(E_s,V_h)$ using\n[[def-eigenspace-best-approximation-gap]]. If $\\delta_s$ is sufficiently\nsmall that the eigenvalue theorem\n[[thm-conforming-galerkin-eigenvalue-upper-bound-quadratic-gap-error]] applies\nat index $s$ and, when $r>1$, at index $r-1$, and\n$C_s\\delta_s^2<\\tfrac12(\\lambda_{s+1}-\\lambda)$ and, when $r>1$,\n$C_{r-1}\\delta_s^2<\\tfrac12(\\lambda-\\lambda_{r-1})$, then\nthe discrete Ritz eigenspace $E_h$ associated with indices $r,\\ldots,s$ has\ndimension $s-r+1$ and\n$$\\delta_a(E,E_h)\\le C_{r,s,\\lambda}\\,\\delta_s.$$\nThis compares the invariant subspaces across the whole repeated eigenvalue;\nit does not select individual eigenvectors inside the cluster.\n\n"
      }
    ]
